{
  "0": "Training strategy: keep the existing optimizer and initial learning rate, but halve the learning rate after two validation checks without HR@10 improvement, with a floor of 0.1 times its initial value. Preserve batch size, dropout, weight decay, early stopping and the 200-epoch cap. Gentler updates after a plateau may reduce the observed validation oscillation without increasing per-step computation.",
  "1": "Learning objective: use 0.9 times the existing loss plus 0.1 times sampled candidate cross-entropy, computed as logsumexp over the positive and existing sampled negative logits minus the positive logit. Reuse the same samples and scoring function; change no optimizer or architecture settings. This auxiliary term may improve competition among candidates and HR@10, particularly for Electronics, while retaining the original objective's influence.",
  "2": "Embedding representation: transform each non-padding input item embedding e to e/sqrt(1+||e||^2) before its existing downstream processing; keep padding zero and leave output embeddings unchanged. This smooth norm bound may prevent frequent items with large embedding norms from dominating GRU gates, improving sparse-item generalization. Preserve embedding dimensions, features, GRU topology, scoring, loss and training settings; reproduce the transform in the predictor writer.",
  "3": "Network architecture: modify each GRU transition to h_t = 0.1*h_previous + 0.9*GRUCell(x_t,h_previous), retaining existing widths, depth and padding behavior. This fixed internal residual path slows hidden-state replacement and may preserve longer-lived interests without adding parameters. Keep embeddings, history selection, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer unchanged, and implement the same recurrence in the generated predictor. Train and load one model per category."
}
